From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 19:38:13 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: enable both USB hosts on the FRITZ!Box 7590

The 7590 populates both of the SoC's USB host controllers where the 7560
populates one, so enable usb0 and usb1 and both of their PHYs. AVM's
HW226 board chain carries no status override on any of the four nodes,
against the 7560's grx350_common.dtsi, which disables usb0.

Port power is the part that is genuinely this board's own. There is one
enable line for both controllers, not one per controller: AVM declares a
single gpio_avm_usb_power_enable on gpio1 2, active high, in
grx550_common.dtsi, with no per-instance variant anywhere in the HW226
chain. gpio1 line 2 is pad 34, and the 7590 board file has no other GPIO
consumer of any kind, so nothing contends for it.

The regulator node is the 7560's, copied unchanged apart from the GPIO,
which is the point: that node form has raised port power on real
hardware, and the boards differ only in which line does it. Both PHYs
name it as their vbus-supply. That is legal and refcounted -- the PHY
driver asks with devm_regulator_get_optional(), which is an
OPTIONAL_GET, and the regulator core only enforces single-consumer
exclusivity for EXCLUSIVE_GET -- so the line rises when the first PHY
powers on and falls when the last one powers off.

As on the 7560, the vendor drives this pad from patched xhci code rather
than as a regulator, and raises it 100 ms after the controller starts
where a vbus-supply raises it before. The 7560's comment records why the
regulator is the better model and that the vendor's delay is the first
thing to try if a device does not enumerate; both apply here unchanged.

This also puts the second instance of the USB PHY through its own
parameters for the first time. usb0 uses a different chip-top word
(+0x18 against +0x34), a different reset bit and a different gate clock
from usb1, and none of those are shared state in the driver -- a wrong
chip-top offset would leave the AXI byte order unset and xHCI would read
its capability registers byte-reversed, and a wrong reset bit would
leave the PHY held in reset. So this board is what shows the
per-instance parameterisation is real rather than incidentally correct
for the one instance the 7560 uses.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index 9ff2a40bb044..7a4e3a009602 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -7,6 +7,8 @@
  */
 /dts-v1/;
 
+#include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+
 #include "seale_avm.dtsi"
 
 / {
@@ -127,6 +129,36 @@ shm-rng-seed@3dfb0000 {
 			no-map;
 		};
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Port power, for both USB host controllers at once. The FRITZ!Box 7560 has
+	 * one port and one enable line; this board has two ports and still
+	 * one enable line. AVM declares a single gpio_avm_usb_power_enable
+	 * on gpio1 2, active high, with no per-controller variant (AVM 4.9
+	 * arch/mips/boot/dts/lantiq/avm/grx550_common.dtsi). That is gpio
+	 * bank 1 line 2, i.e. pad 34, and nothing else on this board claims
+	 * it.
+	 *
+	 * Both PHYs take it as their vbus-supply, which is legal and
+	 * refcounted: the PHY driver asks with regulator_get_optional(), and
+	 * only an exclusive get would turn a second consumer away. Whichever
+	 * PHY powers on first raises the line and it drops again when the
+	 * last one powers off.
+	 *
+	 * The vendor mechanism, and the reason for not reproducing it, are
+	 * the FRITZ!Box 7560's: see the comment on that board's usb_vbus.
+	 *
+	 * No pinmux, for the same reason as there: the pad comes up as a
+	 * GPIO, which is what the regulator needs.
+	 */
+	usb_vbus: regulator-usb-vbus {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "usb_vbus";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <5000000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <5000000>;
+		gpio = <&gpio1 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+	};
 };
 
 /*
@@ -183,3 +215,27 @@ partition@1500000 {
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * Both USB host controllers. The vendor's HW226 chain carries no status
+ * override on either of them, where the FRITZ!Box 7560's grx350_common.dtsi disables
+ * usb0. How many jacks that reaches, and which controller sits behind each
+ * one, is a board-layout fact the vendor sources do not state.
+ */
+&usb0_phy {
+	status = "okay";
+	vbus-supply = <&usb_vbus>;
+};
+
+&usb0 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&usb1_phy {
+	status = "okay";
+	vbus-supply = <&usb_vbus>;
+};
+
+&usb1 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
